/// ============================ TRIPWIRE ==================================
///
/// THE FIRST TEST BELOW PINS BEHAVIOR docs/name-resolution.md SAYS IS WRONG.
/// It passes today because the compiler is wrong today.
///
/// IF YOU ARE HERE BECAUSE IT STARTED FAILING: that is the intended outcome,
/// and it means someone landed the visibility gate. Do NOT delete the
/// assertion to get back to green. Convert this project to `compile_fail`
/// with `expect.diagnostic` E0353, in the same change that made it an error.
///
/// Same trust-loop reasoning as tests/tests/projects/known_fail_canary: a
/// defect that nothing in CI observes is how this subsystem regressed for
/// years (§7's opening premise).
///
/// DO NOT "just switch the gate on" without reading the cryoconfig. That was
/// tried on 2026-08-04: all three binding doors were shut and the compiler
/// and stdlib still self-built clean, but it had to be reverted because the
/// same-module exemption reads the AMBIENT CURSOR, which is wrong often
/// enough to reject calls that never leave their own file. It is blocked on
/// the §5.2 keystone, not on someone noticing.
///
/// ========================================================================
![config(testing)]
namespace ResolutionTripwire::TripwireTest;

import std::core::result;
import std::test::error;
import std::test::assert;

import ResolutionTripwire::Vault;

// ---- KNOWN DEFECT: cross-module `private` call (§3.3, §8.1c) -----------
//
// §3.3: "enforcement is a gate". Today `resolve_qualified_scoped`'s
// single-candidate fast path returns Unique without consulting
// `is_candidate_public`, so a `private` declaration is reachable from
// anywhere as long as it is unambiguous.
//
// §8.1c adds the part that makes this hard: a FUNCTION call is bound by
// sema/call_resolver.cryo, a separate subsystem that never reaches the
// visibility gate at all -- and it has three independent doors.
//
// AFTER THE FIX this must be an E0353 visibility error.

![test]
function WRONG_a_private_function_is_callable_from_another_module()
        -> Result<(), TestError> {
    // `secret` is declared `private` in ResolutionTripwire::Vault.
    // Qualified path: one of the three doors.
    expect_eq(ResolutionTripwire::Vault::secret(), 99)?;
    return Result::Ok(());
}

/// CONTROL, and CORRECT behavior -- must stay green through the fix. If this
/// ever fails alongside the tripwire above, the visibility work broke public
/// access rather than closing the private hole.
![test]
function CONTROL_a_public_function_is_callable_from_another_module()
        -> Result<(), TestError> {
    expect_eq(ResolutionTripwire::Vault::door(), 1)?;
    return Result::Ok(());
}
